  "description": "Junctions and track resources on the track network (the network counterpart of track-connectivity.json and track-resources.json, ARCHITECTURE decision 51). At a node, two edge ends join, so a train may pass from one to the other, when they leave it in opposite directions to within a slope of 1 in 16: their dot product is negative and the cross product at most a sixteenth of its magnitude, in exact integers. A turnout at J (5120, 4096): the stem e1 from W (1024, 4096) and four straight edges east, e2 to E (9216, 4096), e3 to S (9216, 4352), 256 off over 4096, exactly 1 in 16, e4 to U (9216, 5120), 1 in 4, and e5 to V (9216, 3839), 257 off: a train from the stem may take e2 or e3, in ascending order; from e2 or e3 it may go on only to the stem; e4 and e5 join nothing at J. A diamond at X (14336, 4096): e6 from P and e7 to Q cross e8 from R and e9 to T at the node, and each line joins only itself, so no route turns from one to the other. Lengths are round(sqrt(dx^2 + dy^2)): 4104 for e3 and e5, 4222 for e4; track costs 1000 a tile of length, rounded up. A node where edges end cannot be removed (trackNodeInUse). Trains hold resources (Stage S3A): every node their centre line reaches and every span of edge it lies on (an edge of 4096 is four spans of 1024): Local 1 at 2000 along e1 holds 1024-2048, at the span boundary 2048 both spans; Local 2 and Local 3 at X, at the ends of e6 and e8, hold only X, so they conflict there until Local 3 is unplaced.",
